#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:5
msgid "Common Practices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:7
msgid ""
"This section documents common practices when using Scrapy. These are things "
"that cover many topics and don't often fall into any other specific section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:15
msgid "Run Scrapy from a script"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:17
msgid ""
"You can use the :ref:`API <topics-api>` to run Scrapy from a script, instead "
"of the typical way of running Scrapy via ``scrapy crawl``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:20
msgid ""
"Remember that Scrapy requires a Twisted reactor or (with :setting:"
"`TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED` set to ``False``) an asyncio event loop, so you "
"need to run one of those in your script for it to work (helpers described "
"below can do it for you)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:25
msgid ""
"The first utility you can use to run your spiders is :class:`scrapy.crawler."
"AsyncCrawlerProcess` or :class:`scrapy.crawler.CrawlerProcess`. These "
"classes will start a Twisted reactor for you, configuring the logging and "
"setting shutdown handlers. These classes are the ones used by all Scrapy "
"commands. They have similar functionality, differing in their asynchronous "
"API style: :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` returns coroutines "
"from its asynchronous methods while :class:`~scrapy.crawler.CrawlerProcess` "
"returns :class:`~twisted.internet.defer.Deferred` objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:35
msgid "Here's an example showing how to run a single spider with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:37
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"process = AsyncCrawlerProcess(\n"
"    settings={\n"
"        \"FEEDS\": {\n"
"            \"items.json\": {\"format\": \"json\"},\n"
"        },\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"process.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"process.start()  # the script will block here until the crawling is finished"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:59
msgid ""
"You can define :ref:`settings <topics-settings>` within the dictionary "
"passed to :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess`. Make sure to check "
"the :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` documentation to get "
"acquainted with its usage details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:64
msgid ""
"If you are inside a Scrapy project there are some additional helpers you can "
"use to import those components within the project. You can automatically "
"import your spiders passing their name to :class:`~scrapy.crawler."
"AsyncCrawlerProcess`, and use :func:`scrapy.utils.project."
"get_project_settings` to get a :class:`~scrapy.settings.Settings` instance "
"with your project settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:71
msgid ""
"What follows is a working example of how to do that, using the "
"`testspiders`_ project as example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:74
msgid ""
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"from scrapy.utils.project import get_project_settings\n"
"\n"
"process = AsyncCrawlerProcess(get_project_settings())\n"
"\n"
"# 'followall' is the name of one of the spiders of the project.\n"
"process.crawl(\"followall\", domain=\"scrapy.org\")\n"
"process.start()  # the script will block here until the crawling is finished"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:85
msgid ""
"There's another Scrapy utility that provides more control over the crawling "
"process: :class:`scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` or :class:`scrapy."
"crawler.CrawlerRunner`. These classes are thin wrappers that encapsulate "
"some simple helpers to run multiple crawlers, but they won't start or "
"interfere with existing reactors in any way. Just like :class:`scrapy."
"crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` and :class:`scrapy.crawler.CrawlerProcess` they "
"differ in their asynchronous API style."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:94
msgid ""
"When using these classes the reactor should be explicitly run after "
"scheduling your spiders. It's recommended that you use :class:`~scrapy."
"crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` or :class:`~scrapy.crawler.CrawlerRunner` "
"instead of :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` or :class:`~scrapy."
"crawler.CrawlerProcess` if your application is already using Twisted and you "
"want to run Scrapy in the same reactor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:102
msgid ""
"If you want to stop the reactor or run any other code right after the spider "
"finishes you can do that after the task returned from :meth:"
"`AsyncCrawlerRunner.crawl() <scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner.crawl>` "
"completes (or the Deferred returned from :meth:`CrawlerRunner.crawl() "
"<scrapy.crawler.CrawlerRunner.crawl>` fires). In the simplest case you can "
"also use :func:`twisted.internet.task.react` to start and stop the reactor, "
"though it may be easier to just use :class:`~scrapy.crawler."
"AsyncCrawlerProcess` or :class:`~scrapy.crawler.CrawlerProcess` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:111
msgid ""
"Here's an example of using :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` "
"together with simple reactor management code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:114
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.defer import deferred_f_from_coro_f\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"from scrapy.utils.reactor import install_reactor\n"
"from twisted.internet.task import react\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def crawl(_):\n"
"    configure_logging({\"LOG_FORMAT\": \"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\"})\n"
"    runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner()\n"
"    await runner.crawl(MySpider)  # completes when the spider finishes\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"install_reactor(\"twisted.internet.asyncioreactor.AsyncioSelectorReactor\")\n"
"react(deferred_f_from_coro_f(crawl))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:138
msgid ""
"Same example but using :class:`~scrapy.crawler.CrawlerRunner` and a "
"different reactor (:class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` only works "
"with :class:`~twisted.internet.asyncioreactor.AsyncioSelectorReactor`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:142
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import CrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"from scrapy.utils.reactor import install_reactor\n"
"from twisted.internet.task import react\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    custom_settings = {\n"
"        \"TWISTED_REACTOR\": \"twisted.internet.epollreactor."
"EPollReactor\",\n"
"    }\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def crawl(_):\n"
"    configure_logging({\"LOG_FORMAT\": \"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\"})\n"
"    runner = CrawlerRunner()\n"
"    d = runner.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"    return d  # this Deferred fires when the spider finishes\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"install_reactor(\"twisted.internet.epollreactor.EPollReactor\")\n"
"react(crawl)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:169
msgid ":doc:`twisted:core/howto/reactor-basics`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:171
msgid ""
"And here are examples of using these classes with :setting:"
"`TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED` set to ``False``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:174
msgid "Simple usage of :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:176
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"process = AsyncCrawlerProcess(\n"
"    settings={\n"
"        \"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": False,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"process.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"process.start()  # the script will block here until the crawling is finished"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:196
msgid ""
"With ``TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED=False`` you can use several instances of :"
"class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` in the same process:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:199
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"process1 = AsyncCrawlerProcess(\n"
"    settings={\n"
"        \"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": False,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"process1.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"process1.start()\n"
"\n"
"process2 = AsyncCrawlerProcess(\n"
"    settings={\n"
"        \"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": False,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"process2.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"process2.start()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:226
msgid ""
"Using :func:`asyncio.run` with :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:228
msgid ""
"import asyncio\n"
"\n"
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def main():\n"
"    configure_logging({\"LOG_FORMAT\": \"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\"})\n"
"    runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner(settings={\"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": "
"False})\n"
"    await runner.crawl(MySpider)  # completes when the spider finishes\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"asyncio.run(main())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:253
msgid "Running spiders inside existing applications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:255
msgid ""
"You may want to run Scrapy spiders inside an existing application. In simple "
"cases (e.g. task queues that spawn a process for every task, or applications "
"that can execute tasks synchronously in the same process) you can use the "
"same approach as for standalone scripts (see :ref:`run-from-script`). More "
"complex cases, e.g. asynchronous web applications, have additional caveats "
"and limitations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:262
msgid ""
"If the application runs its own Twisted reactor, you can use :class:`~scrapy."
"crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` or :class:`~scrapy.crawler.CrawlerRunner` to run "
"spiders using this reactor, see :ref:`run-from-script` for examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:267
msgid ""
"If the application doesn't run a Twisted reactor or an asyncio event loop "
"(for example, a Django web app deployed with a WSGI server such as uWSGI), "
"you can use :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerProcess` with :setting:"
"`TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED` set to ``False``, so that Scrapy starts and stops "
"an asyncio event loop for every spider run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:273
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from django.http import HttpResponse\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def crawl_view(request):\n"
"    process = AsyncCrawlerProcess(settings={\"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": "
"False})\n"
"    process.crawl(MySpider)\n"
"    process.start()  # returns when the spider finishes\n"
"    return HttpResponse(\"Crawling finished\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:291
msgid ""
"If the application runs its own asyncio event loop (for example, a Django "
"web app deployed with an ASGI server such as uvicorn), you can use :class:"
"`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` with :setting:`TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED` "
"set to ``False``, so that Scrapy uses the existing event loop:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:297
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from django.http import HttpResponse\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def crawl_view(request):\n"
"    runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner(settings={\"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": "
"False})\n"
"    await runner.crawl(MySpider)  # completes when the spider finishes\n"
"    return HttpResponse(\"Crawling finished\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:314 ../../../topics/practices.rst:352
msgid ""
"Running Scrapy without a Twisted reactor is experimental and has some "
"limitations, described in :ref:`asyncio-without-reactor`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:320
msgid "Running spiders in Jupyter notebooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:322
msgid ""
"You can run Scrapy spiders in Jupyter notebooks. You need to use :class:"
"`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` with :setting:`TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED` "
"set to ``False`` for this, so that Scrapy uses the event loop provided by "
"the notebook kernel. As :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner` doesn't "
"configure logging, and you most likely want to see the spider log in the "
"notebook, you should call :func:`scrapy.utils.log.configure_logging`. Here "
"is a full example, which supports rerunning both as a single cell and as "
"separate cells:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:331
msgid ""
"from scrapy import Spider\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"\n"
"configure_logging()\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class BooksSpider(Spider):\n"
"    name = \"books\"\n"
"    start_urls = [\"https://books.toscrape.com\"]\n"
"\n"
"    def parse(self, response):\n"
"        for book in response.css(\"h3\"):\n"
"            yield {\"title\": book.css(\"a::attr(title)\").get()}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner({\"TWISTED_REACTOR_ENABLED\": False})\n"
"await runner.crawl(BooksSpider)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:358
msgid "Running multiple spiders in the same process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:360
msgid ""
"By default, Scrapy runs a single spider per process when you run ``scrapy "
"crawl``. However, Scrapy supports running multiple spiders per process using "
"the :ref:`internal API <topics-api>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:364
msgid "Here is an example that runs multiple spiders simultaneously:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:366
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerProcess\n"
"from scrapy.utils.project import get_project_settings\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider1(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your first spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider2(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your second spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"settings = get_project_settings()\n"
"process = AsyncCrawlerProcess(settings)\n"
"process.crawl(MySpider1)\n"
"process.crawl(MySpider2)\n"
"process.start()  # the script will block here until all crawling jobs are "
"finished"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:389
msgid "Same example using :class:`~scrapy.crawler.AsyncCrawlerRunner`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:391
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.defer import deferred_f_from_coro_f\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"from scrapy.utils.reactor import install_reactor\n"
"from twisted.internet.task import react\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider1(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your first spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider2(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your second spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def crawl(_):\n"
"    configure_logging({\"LOG_FORMAT\": \"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\"})\n"
"    runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner()\n"
"    runner.crawl(MySpider1)\n"
"    runner.crawl(MySpider2)\n"
"    await runner.join()  # completes when both spiders finish\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"install_reactor(\"twisted.internet.asyncioreactor.AsyncioSelectorReactor\")\n"
"react(deferred_f_from_coro_f(crawl))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:423
msgid ""
"Same example but running the spiders sequentially by awaiting until each one "
"finishes before starting the next one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:426
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"from scrapy.crawler import AsyncCrawlerRunner\n"
"from scrapy.utils.defer import deferred_f_from_coro_f\n"
"from scrapy.utils.log import configure_logging\n"
"from scrapy.utils.reactor import install_reactor\n"
"from twisted.internet.task import react\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider1(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your first spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class MySpider2(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    # Your second spider definition\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def crawl(_):\n"
"    configure_logging({\"LOG_FORMAT\": \"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\"})\n"
"    runner = AsyncCrawlerRunner()\n"
"    await runner.crawl(MySpider1)\n"
"    await runner.crawl(MySpider2)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"install_reactor(\"twisted.internet.asyncioreactor.AsyncioSelectorReactor\")\n"
"react(deferred_f_from_coro_f(crawl))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:456
msgid ""
"When running multiple spiders in the same process, :ref:`logging settings "
"<logging-settings>` and :ref:`reactor settings <reactor-settings>` should "
"not have a different value per spider, and :ref:`pre-crawler settings <pre-"
"crawler-settings>` cannot be defined per spider."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:461
msgid ""
"Every other setting applies to each crawler separately. This includes "
"concurrency and politeness settings, such as :setting:"
"`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS`, :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS_PER_DOMAIN` and :"
"setting:`DOWNLOAD_DELAY`, and :ref:`AutoThrottle <topics-autothrottle>` also "
"throttles each crawler separately. When crawling simultaneously, divide "
"those values by the number of crawlers to keep the combined load on your "
"hardware and on target websites unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:469
msgid ""
"Because of this, running the same spider several times in the same process "
"multiplies those limits instead of increasing crawling capacity. To crawl "
"faster, raise :setting:`CONCURRENT_REQUESTS` on a single crawler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:473
msgid ":ref:`run-from-script`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:480
msgid "Distributed crawls"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:482
msgid ""
"Scrapy doesn't provide any built-in facility for running crawls in a "
"distributed (multi-server) manner. However, there are some ways to "
"distribute crawls, which vary depending on how you plan to distribute them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:486
msgid ""
"If you have many spiders, the obvious way to distribute the load is to setup "
"many Scrapyd instances and distribute spider runs among those."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:489
msgid ""
"If you instead want to run a single (big) spider through many machines, what "
"you usually do is partition the URLs to crawl and send them to each separate "
"spider. Here is a concrete example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:493
msgid ""
"First, you prepare the list of URLs to crawl and put them into separate "
"files/urls::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:496
msgid ""
"http://somedomain.com/urls-to-crawl/spider1/part1.list\n"
"http://somedomain.com/urls-to-crawl/spider1/part2.list\n"
"http://somedomain.com/urls-to-crawl/spider1/part3.list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:500
msgid ""
"Then you fire a spider run on 3 different Scrapyd servers. The spider would "
"receive a (spider) argument ``part`` with the number of the partition to "
"crawl::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:504
msgid ""
"curl http://scrapy1.mycompany.com:6800/schedule.json -d project=myproject -d "
"spider=spider1 -d part=1\n"
"curl http://scrapy2.mycompany.com:6800/schedule.json -d project=myproject -d "
"spider=spider1 -d part=2\n"
"curl http://scrapy3.mycompany.com:6800/schedule.json -d project=myproject -d "
"spider=spider1 -d part=3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:511
msgid "Reducing startup time in large projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:513
msgid ""
"When running a spider with ``scrapy crawl``, Scrapy loads all modules listed "
"in :setting:`SPIDER_MODULES` to find the target spider. In large projects "
"with many spiders, this can noticeably increase startup time and memory "
"usage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:517
msgid ""
"To avoid loading every spider module, override :setting:`SPIDER_MODULES` on "
"the command line to point only to the module that contains the spider you "
"want to run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:521
msgid "scrapy crawl myspider -s SPIDER_MODULES=myproject.spiders.myspider"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:525
msgid ""
"Because :setting:`SPIDER_MODULES` is a list setting, you can include "
"multiple modules by separating them with commas."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:531
msgid "Avoiding getting banned"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:533
msgid ""
"Websites tell regular visitors and crawlers apart by how their traffic "
"looks: the headers it carries, how fast it arrives, how many requests come "
"from the same place. Traffic that stands out can be blocked even when the "
"crawling itself would be welcome."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:538
msgid ""
"Where the website allows crawling, the most effective thing you can do is "
"make yourself known: set :setting:`USER_AGENT` to a value that identifies "
"you and lets its owners reach you, so that they can ask you to adjust your "
"crawler rather than block it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:543
msgid ""
"Where that is not enough, the following make your traffic resemble that of a "
"regular visitor:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:546
msgid ""
"rotate your user agent among those of common browsers, so that your requests "
"do not all look alike (search the web for an up-to-date list)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:548
msgid ""
"disable cookies (see :setting:`COOKIES_ENABLED`), so that a session "
"identifier does not tie all your requests together"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:550
msgid ""
"space out your requests, 2 seconds apart or more, with the :setting:"
"`DOWNLOAD_DELAY` setting, to keep your pace closer to that of a person "
"browsing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:553
msgid ""
"where possible, read pages from `Common Crawl`_, which sends no traffic to "
"the website at all"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:555
msgid ""
"spread your requests over a pool of IP addresses, so that none of them "
"accounts for your whole crawl. For example, the free `Tor project`_ or paid "
"services like `ProxyMesh`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:558
msgid ""
"match the TLS behavior of a browser: some websites respond differently "
"depending on the TLS version of the client, which you can adjust with the :"
"setting:`DOWNLOAD_TLS_MIN_VERSION` and :setting:`DOWNLOAD_TLS_MAX_VERSION` "
"settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:562
msgid ""
"let a service take care of all of the above, such as `Zyte API`_, which "
"provides a `Scrapy plugin <https://github.com/scrapy-plugins/scrapy-zyte-"
"api>`__ and additional features, like `AI web scraping <https://www.zyte.com/"
"ai-web-scraping/>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/practices.rst:567
msgid ""
"If your crawler still gets blocked, consider contacting `commercial "
"support`_."
msgstr ""

